WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.623
This S3 cache

00:00:01.458 --> 00:00:02.177
is full of

00:00:01.989 --> 00:00:03.475
holes on purpose

00:00:04.014 --> 00:00:04.756
Berserk

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:05.664
a data store

00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:06.301
for logs

00:00:06.349 --> 00:00:07.870
metrics traces and

00:00:07.705 --> 00:00:08.436
evals

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.004
caches S3 data

00:00:09.816 --> 00:00:10.888
on query nodes

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:11.926
in sparse files

00:00:12.454 --> 00:00:13.727
Each node stores

00:00:13.551 --> 00:00:14.494
only the pieces

00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:15.331
it needs and

00:00:15.143 --> 00:00:16.380
punches holes to

00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:17.548
reclaim disk space

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:18.680
as pieces fall

00:00:18.492 --> 00:00:19.376
out of use

00:00:19.515 --> 00:00:20.493
A file can

00:00:20.305 --> 00:00:21.366
look huge while

00:00:21.178 --> 00:00:22.404
using little disk

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:24.325
Workers keep several

00:00:24.137 --> 00:00:25.492
S3 requests in

00:00:25.304 --> 00:00:25.882
flight

00:00:26.153 --> 00:00:27.191
read ahead and

00:00:27.014 --> 00:00:28.158
read directly from

00:00:27.969 --> 00:00:29.089
the shared cache

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:29.935
It's a trick

00:00:29.747 --> 00:00:30.749
the author first

00:00:30.561 --> 00:00:31.350
learned as a

00:00:31.162 --> 00:00:33.001
sysadmin in 1990

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:34.548
Full story on

00:00:34.359 --> 00:00:35.255
the dashboard
